{#- console-roles-head.njk — decide the nav BEFORE the header paints

   Goes in <head>, deliberately, and is inline rather than a file: it has to
   run before the browser reaches the header, and a <script src> can be
   delayed long enough to lose that race.

   THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES. Rows used to be revealed by staff.js after the page
   had painted, and again when the identity fetch returned — so a two-row
   account watched the nav appear, then grow, on every single page. This
   settles it from a cached identity before anything is drawn, which is the
   same trick staff-i18n.js already uses to stop the console rendering in
   English and correcting itself.

   IT SETS CLASSES, NOT STYLES. CSS decides what a role can see; this only says
   which roles are in play. staff.js sets the same classes again when the real
   identity arrives, so a stale cache self-corrects within one request — and a
   correct one changes nothing, which is the point.

   `data-area` is the fallback for a first-ever visit with nothing cached:
   show the console this page belongs to rather than an empty header. -#}
<script>
(function () {
  var el = document.documentElement;
  el.setAttribute('data-area', '{{ "admin" if adminPage else "staff" }}');
  try {
    var who = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem('thauma.staff.who') || 'null');
    if (who && who.roles && who.roles.length) {
      for (var i = 0; i < who.roles.length; i++) {
        el.classList.add('role-' + String(who.roles[i]).replace(/[^a-z]/gi, ''));
      }
      el.classList.add('roles-known');
    }
  } catch (e) { /* no storage, or nothing cached: data-area carries it */ }
})();
</script>

{#- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   AND THE HEIGHT IT ENDED UP BEING.

   `--header-h` was a hard-coded 62px describing `.top`, the header this
   redesign replaced. `.top` is no longer rendered anywhere, so the number
   has been describing nothing since — and three live rules still read it:
   the persistent problem toast, the sticky position of an open editor row,
   and the sign-in gate's height.

   For a one-row account 62px was close enough to hide the error. For an
   admin+staff account the real header is about 94px, so all three sat
   roughly fifty pixels too high: the problem toast covered the second nav
   row completely, and with no way to dismiss it the account that hit it
   could not navigate at all.

   IT IS MEASURED, NOT CALCULATED. Which rows appear depends on five roles
   in combination — communications alone shows both — and a CSS selector
   for that would be nine rules that drift the first time the matrix
   changes. The element knows its own height; asking it cannot be wrong.

   A ResizeObserver rather than a one-off read, because the header changes
   height after the fact: staff.js re-sets the role classes when the real
   identity arrives, which is exactly the case that was broken. -#}
<script>
(function () {
  function measure(el) {
    var h = el.getBoundingClientRect().height;
    if (h > 0) document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--header-h', Math.round(h) + 'px');
  }
  function start() {
    var el = document.querySelector('.console');
    if (!el) return;
    measure(el);
    if (window.ResizeObserver) new ResizeObserver(function () { measure(el); }).observe(el);
    else window.addEventListener('resize', function () { measure(el); });
  }
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', start);
  } else { start(); }
})();
</script>
